\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Two hardening runs (C07). Top: 19 squares; disks are compressed, harden into squares, and settle into Wainwright's packing (side $3+4\sqrt  2/3$), which no other method reached in 32 runs. Bottom: the best of 32 hardening runs for 12 triangles in a triangle ends at $3.915$, while rigid and snap starts reach the best known $3.879$; the triangles that emerge from the near-hexagonal disk arrangement keep orientations that do not tile. Every replay is on the project site.}}{6}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:strip}{{1}{6}{Two hardening runs (C07). Top: 19 squares; disks are compressed, harden into squares, and settle into Wainwright's packing (side $3+4\sqrt 2/3$), which no other method reached in 32 runs. Bottom: the best of 32 hardening runs for 12 triangles in a triangle ends at $3.915$, while rigid and snap starts reach the best known $3.879$; the triangles that emerge from the near-hexagonal disk arrangement keep orientations that do not tile. Every replay is on the project site}{figure.1}{}}
\newlabel{fig:tristrip}{{1}{6}{Two hardening runs (C07). Top: 19 squares; disks are compressed, harden into squares, and settle into Wainwright's packing (side $3+4\sqrt 2/3$), which no other method reached in 32 runs. Bottom: the best of 32 hardening runs for 12 triangles in a triangle ends at $3.915$, while rigid and snap starts reach the best known $3.879$; the triangles that emerge from the near-hexagonal disk arrangement keep orientations that do not tile. Every replay is on the project site}{figure.1}{}}
